\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Verified affine escape of outgoing radial null rays in the exact Schwarzschild exterior; no generic collapse or censorship theorem follows.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied definition, catalogue wording, source qualifications and reference entries for 30003910. The historical source/context and inherited status are retained; no fresh literature or novelty certification is claimed. No external theorem is used without proof in this attempt; explicit model assumptions are distinguished from facts about the source model.

\paragraph{An exact exterior escape calculation.} For the four-dimensional Schwarzschild exterior with parameter \(M>0\), take
\[
 ds^2=-f(r)dt^2+f(r)^{-1}dr^2+r^2d\Omega^2,\qquad f(r)=1-2M/r,\quad r>2M.
\]
For a radial null geodesic the conserved quantity \(E=f\dot t>0\), together with the null equation, gives \(\dot r=E\) on the outgoing branch; the dot denotes an affine parameter derivative. Hence \(r=r_0+E(\lambda-\lambda_0)\), and reaching arbitrarily large radius requires an unbounded affine interval. Coordinate time obeys
\[
 t-t_0=r-r_0+2M\log\frac{r-2M}{r_0-2M},
\]
obtained by integrating \(dt/dr=f^{-1}\). Every outgoing ray starting at \(r_0>2M\) escapes, and the divergence as \(r_0\downarrow2M\) is a coordinate-time effect in this formula.
\paragraph{Why this is insufficient.} This verifies one elementary exterior completeness mechanism for an exact metric. It does not construct a collapse spacetime, control all nonradial geodesics, establish conformal null infinity, or prove persistence under perturbations. In particular it cannot exclude a singularity whose evolving exterior ceases to resemble this metric. The source asks for appropriate geometric/dimensional hypotheses and genericity, none of which is fixed by this calculation. The first missing step is an a priori global exterior estimate for a specified evolution and data class. No statement that censorship holds for every matter model or every asymptotically flat data set is inferred.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked affine versus coordinate time, r0 strictly outside 2M and the limitation to outgoing radial rays in an exact metric. This is a GPT-6 Astra Ultra self-audit, not independent human verification. Prompt A drafting followed by prompt B scope/dependency/boundary self-audit. Checked affine versus coordinate time, r0 strictly outside 2M and the limitation to outgoing radial rays in an exact metric. The remaining gap is: Prove global exterior estimates and the appropriate null-infinity completeness for a specified generic asymptotically flat evolution class. Unresolved status and zero original-target completion are retained.

Coordinator checked the radial null-geodesic first integrals, affine escape and coordinate-time integral. Global censorship conclusions are not inferred. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

\paragraph{Tools, process and contributions.}
Python 3 and local file-reading/writing tools for manuscript serialization and structural checks; mathematical calculations were symbolic reasoning, with no numerical experiment or theorem prover. Contribution prompt A was followed by prompt B. GPT-6 Astra with ultra reasoning drafted the research and performed its stated self-audit; any additional coordinator contribution is identified in the verification and audit. A GPT-6-based Codex coordinator assembled the manuscript and checked repository integration; its exact variant and reasoning configuration were not exposed. No human mathematical contribution, independent human proof check or formal proof certification is supplied. Complete derivations and the consulted source references are included here; private reasoning transcripts are not published.

\paragraph{Final status and first remaining gap.}
\textbf{UNRESOLVED}. Prove global exterior estimates and the appropriate null-infinity completeness for a specified generic asymptotically flat evolution class.

